  "question": {
    "text": "Two non-conducting spheres of radii <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;and carrying uniform volume charge densities&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mi>&#961;</mi></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mi>&#961;</mi></math>,&nbsp;respectively, are placed such that they partially overlap, as shown in the figure. At all points in the overlapping region,<br /><u><strong><img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\qt0031_a6d6f455463c.jpg\" style=\"width: 167px; height: 124px;\" /></strong></u>"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "the electrostatic field is zero.",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "the electrostatic potential is constant.",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "the electrostatic field is constant in magnitude.",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "the electrostatic field has same direction.",
      "is_correct": true
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "C",
    "D"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>In triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>,<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mover><mi>r</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mover><mi>d</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><msub><mover><mi>r</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></math></p><p><br /><img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\st0031_7e163fb7049b.jpg\" style=\"width: 164px; height: 135px;\" /></p><p>The electrostatic field at point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> is,<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi>E</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>K</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi>&#961;</mi><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfenced><mfenced><msub><mover><mi>r</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub></mfenced></mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>K</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi>&#961;</mi><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfenced><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><msub><mover><mi>r</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mfrac></math><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi>E</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi><mi>&#961;</mi><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mfenced><mrow><msub><mover><mi>r</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mover><mi>r</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi>E</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>&#961;</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>&#949;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mover><mi>d</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover></math></p>"
  }
